# ---------------- P1 ----------------
add("P1","",pad("<div class='fill'>"+R(0,"<h1>NeuroLens</h1>")+R(1,"<div class='tag hl' style='font-size:64px'>Watch a brain watch your video.</div>")+R(2,"<p class='mono' style='margin-top:48px'>Predicted brain response to video, second by second</p>")+R(3,"<p class='mono' style='margin-top:14px'>CS5224 Cloud Computing · Final presentation · Dylan Chan · Xin Wei Chong · Kelvin Low · Joshua Lum · Brandon Tan</p>")+"</div>","title")+CURVE,
"Good afternoon, everyone, and thank you for joining us. We're team NeuroLens. Over the next fifteen minutes we'll show you a cloud service that sounds a little like science fiction: it predicts how a viewer's brain responds to a video, second by second, without putting anyone inside a scanner. The curve behind me is exactly what our model produces: one reading for every second of a video. I'll set up the problem and the idea. Then my teammates will run the whole thing live, explain the business model, and share how we tested it. Everything you'll see today is running on AWS right now, and you can try it yourself at the address on the last slide.","title")
add("P1","Problem",pad("<div class='fill'><div style='display:grid;grid-template-columns:660px 1fr;gap:70px;align-items:center'>"+R(0,"<div class='big' style='font-size:250px'>$10k+</div><p class='sub' style='margin-top:30px;font-size:40px'>to test one ad on a brain scanner</p>")+R(1,"<div class='rows'><div class='r'><span>fMRI study</span><span>$10 – 15k</span><span>2–4 weeks</span></div><div class='r'><span>Facial coding</span><span>$850 – 4,000</span><span>3–5 days</span></div><div class='r'><span>AI attention maps</span><span>$0.5–1.5k/mo</span><span>sub-minute</span></div><div class='r us'><span>NeuroLens</span><span>$1.00</span><span>≈ 7 min</span></div></div>")+"</div>"+R(2,"<p class='src'>Cost per advert or cut. fMRI vs surveys: Venkatraman et al., 2015. Competitor ranges: our report, section 1.3. NeuroLens: $1.00 per 30 s video; about 7 minutes measured for a 30 s clip.</p>")+"</div>"),
"Let's start with a simple question: how do you know whether an ad works? Surveys are the usual answer, but people are bad at explaining their own reactions. Brain scans do better, because they show how viewers really react, second by second. The catch is the price. An fMRI study costs ten to fifteen thousand dollars per advert and takes two to four weeks. Webcam facial coding is cheaper, between eight hundred fifty and four thousand dollars a cut, but takes three to five business days and only sees faces. AI attention maps are quick and cheap, but they are heuristic heatmaps, not a brain response. The last row is NeuroLens: one dollar for a thirty-second video, and about seven minutes, which we measured. That gap is the whole reason this project exists.")
add("P1","Why now",pad("<div style='display:grid;grid-template-columns:960px 1fr;gap:40px;flex:1;align-items:center'><div>"+R(0,"<h1 style='font-size:80px'>Ten cuts by lunch.<br><em>No way to judge them.</em></h1>")+R(1,"<p class='sub'>Generative tools made production cheap. The feed made every second count.</p>")+"</div>"+R(2,"<svg viewBox='0 0 800 520' width='800' height='520'><line x1='0' y1='290' x2='800' y2='290' stroke='#262A31' stroke-width='2' stroke-dasharray='8 12'/><path class='draw' pathLength='1' stroke='#FF8A2A' stroke-width='6' d='M0 120 C100 110 170 100 250 140 S340 170 390 290 S480 450 540 420 S650 300 720 350 S780 400 800 450'/><circle cx='505' cy='430' r='26' fill='none' stroke='#FFC24A' stroke-width='4'/><text x='440' y='500' fill='#FFC24A' font-family='JetBrains Mono' font-size='26'>where did we lose them?</text><text x='0' y='40' fill='#8B929C' font-family='JetBrains Mono' font-size='24'>VIEWER ATTENTION (ILLUSTRATIVE)</text></svg>")+"</div>"),
"And the gap is getting wider. Generative tools like Runway, Sora and CapCut mean a small team can now produce ten versions of an ad before lunch. Every one of those versions is something someone has to judge. Meanwhile, viewers watch in fast-scrolling feeds, where one swipe ends the ad. So it's not enough to have a good hook: you can lose someone at an awkward cut, or in a quiet stretch in the middle, like this dip on the attention curve. The question every creator is asking is: where exactly did I lose them, and which version is stronger? Production got cheap, but evaluation is still slow, expensive, and mostly guesswork.")
add("P1","Idea",pad("<div class='fill'>"+R(0,"<h1>One upload in.<br><em>A brain response out.</em></h1>")+"<div class='flow reveal' style='grid-template-columns:repeat(4,1fr);margin-top:130px;--d:2'><div class='n'><b>Your video</b><span>MP4, MOV or WebM, up to two minutes</span></div><div class='n'><b>TRIBE v2</b><span>Meta's brain model, trained on real fMRI of people watching film</span></div><div class='n'><b>20,484 points</b><span>of the cortex, predicted every second</span></div><div class='n'><b>Three outputs</b><span>Timeline · 3D brain · AI report</span></div></div>"+R(3,"<p class='sub' style='margin-top:90px;font-size:34px'>For every second of the video. A virtual pre-test of a typical viewer, not a prediction of views or sales.</p>")+"</div>"),
"So here's NeuroLens, in one line. You upload a video. It goes into TRIBE v2, a research model from Meta that predicts brain activity from video. Under the hood, WhisperX turns the speech into words, LLaMA 3.2 reads them, Wav2Vec-BERT listens to the sound and V-JEPA 2 watches the frames. For every second, it predicts activity at twenty thousand four hundred and eighty-four points on the cortex. We average that over brain areas from the Glasser atlas to get seven signals in three groups, and we do it twice, once with the sound and once muted. A creator gets three things: a timeline you can scrub, a 3D brain synced to the video, and a written report. This is a virtual pre-test, not a prediction of views or sales. I'll hand over to my teammate.")
# ---------------- P2 ----------------
add("P2","Users",pad("<div class='fill'>"+R(0,"<h1>Two people, <em>two questions.</em></h1>")+"<div class='cols c2' style='margin-top:110px'>"+R(1,"<div class='q'>Which three seconds do I cut?</div><p>A short-form creator uploads two edits of an intro, finds the dip, and re-cuts.</p>")+R(2,"<div class='q'>Which variant gets the media budget?</div><p>A small agency uploads up to five cuts, compares any pair, and decides with evidence.</p>")+"</div></div>"),
"Thanks. Who is this for? Meet two people. Maya makes short videos. Her question is: which three seconds do I cut? She uploads two edits of her intro, the curve dips at her cold open, the report names the second, and she re-cuts and compares. The second is a four-person agency with three variants of a client's ad and one media budget. They upload all their cuts, compare any pair, and the verdict says where each one wins, so the decision rests on evidence rather than a hunch. The service supports batch upload of up to five files, and a written comparison for any two finished videos. These are personas, but the workflows are exactly what we'll show you live.")
add("P2","Signals",pad("<div class='fill'>"+R(0,"<h1>Seven brain areas.<br><em>Three questions.</em></h1>")+"<div class='cols c3' style='margin-top:100px;gap:80px'>"+R(1,"<h2 class='hl'>Seeing</h2><p>faces · bodies · scenes · visual</p>")+R(2,"<h2 class='hl2'>Hearing</h2><p>sound</p>")+R(3,"<h2>Understanding</h2><p>speech · social</p>")+"</div>"+R(4,"<div style='margin-top:90px;height:10px;border-radius:5px;background:linear-gradient(90deg,#2a3a55,#2a2f38 45%,#6b3a12 60%,#FF8A2A 80%,#FFC24A)'></div><div style='display:flex;justify-content:space-between;margin-top:14px' class='mono'><span>below typical</span><span>0 = typical film &amp; TV viewing</span><span>above typical</span></div>")+"</div>"),
"Here's what the service actually measures. Each signal is the average predicted activity in a set of brain areas from the Glasser atlas, and we group the seven signals into three simple ideas. Seeing covers faces, bodies, scenes, and visual areas. Hearing covers sound. Understanding covers speech and social interaction. Every second is shown against typical film and TV viewing: zero is a typical moment, above means a stronger-than-typical response, and any two videos sit on the same scale, so they can be compared directly. We also run the video a second time with the sound removed, which shows how much of the response comes from the soundtrack. In the demo, you'll see that for one ad it's almost half.")
add("P2","Journey",pad("<div class='fill'>"+R(0,"<h1>From file to insight in <em>five steps.</em></h1>")+"<div class='flow reveal' style='grid-template-columns:repeat(5,1fr);margin-top:150px;--d:2'>"+''.join(f"<div class='n'><i>0{k+1}</i><b>{a}</b><span>{b}</span></div>" for k,(a,b) in enumerate([("Sign in","Google or email · $10 invite credit"),("Upload","$1.00 per started 30 s, shown first"),("Predict","A GPU starts on demand"),("Watch","Live progress, stage by stage"),("Explore","Curve · 3D brain · report · compare")]))+"</div>"+R(3,"<p class='mono' style='margin-top:70px'>MP4, MOV, WebM · up to 2 minutes · $1.00 per started 30 s of video</p>")+"</div>"),
"The journey has five steps. You sign in with Google, or with an email and password. New users get ten dollars of invite credit, enough for ten thirty-second videos. You upload a video, MP4, MOV or WebM up to two minutes. The price is one dollar for each started thirty seconds, shown before you commit, with a short window to cancel. A GPU starts on demand and the model scores every second, with live progress, stage by stage. Then you explore: the timeline, the 3D brain, the AI report, and side-by-side comparison. Rather than describe it any further, let me show you. I'm going to do this live.")
add("P2","Demo 1",dtitle("Sign in, upload, watch it process")+shot("app_home",140,220,1290,"The upload screen")+cues(["Sign in with Google or email","The price shows before you confirm","Stages tick: check, queue, analyse"]),
"I'm switching to the live site now. This is the address on the screen, and it's running on AWS in the cloud, not on my laptop. I'll sign in with my Google account. Once I'm in, you can see my credit balance at the top, and the upload area here. I'll choose this thirty-second clip. Before anything is sent, the app tells me exactly how much credit this will use, and I get a short window to cancel. That matters, because GPU time costs real money. I'll confirm. The file goes straight from my browser to cloud storage. Now the job moves through stages: checking the file, waiting for a GPU, and analysing. We warmed a GPU up before this session, because a cold start takes about six minutes. Even warm, a thirty-second clip takes about seven minutes to finish, so I'll leave it running and hand over to my colleague, who'll show you a finished result.","demo")
# ---------------- P3 ----------------
add("P3","Demo 2",dtitle("The results, second by second")+shot("result",140,220,1290,"Free sample · CeraVe ad, 30 s")+cues(["Follow the playhead","Peak near 0:06, dip at the end","Seeing · hearing · understanding","Toggle: without sound"]),
"Thanks. Here's a finished analysis, the thirty-second CeraVe ad, one of our free sample videos. On the left, the video plays, and everything on the right follows it. This orange line is the overall predicted brain response, compared with typical viewing. Watch the playhead: right around second six, when the actor holds up the product and looks into the camera, the line peaks, and the label reads well above typical. The orange dots are moments the system has flagged as notable. Underneath, the response is split into seeing, hearing, and understanding, all on the same scale, so you can see which channel drives the peak. Here it's seeing and hearing together, the face and the voice. Towards the end the line falls below typical. That's the closing product card, with no face and no speech. And this toggle shows the same video without sound. The page tells us forty-six percent of the response comes from the soundtrack. On the left, the 3D brain is synced to the video, and you can drag it to rotate.","demo")
add("P3","Demo 3",dtitle("The AI report, and comparing two cuts")+shot("report",140,300,780,"Report tab")+shot("compare",1000,300,780,"Compare tab · 30 s cut vs 76 s cut"),
"Numbers are useful, but creators want advice. This Report tab is written by an AI model, which reads the brain data and the video frames together. Here it says: a strong celebrity-driven open, with the board-room wide shots and the long product card as the two opportunities. It lists strengths, improvements, and then specific moments with the second each one starts, what's on screen, and what the brain is doing. For agencies, the Compare tab puts two videos side by side. Here's the thirty-second cut against the extended cut of the same ad. The verdict says the short cut's direct opening gives it an early edge, but the two finish close. That's the pre-publish decision in one screen.","demo")
add("P3","Demo 4",dtitle("A bad upload costs nothing")+shot("app_home",140,220,1290,"Rejected before any GPU starts")+cues(["Upload a text file renamed .mp4","It is rejected within seconds","No credit is charged"]),
"One more thing people always ask: what if someone uploads something that isn't a valid video? I'll try a plain text file renamed to MP4. It's checked before any GPU is started, so it's rejected within seconds, and no credit is charged. That's both a safety measure and a cost control. Failed analyses are also refunded automatically, so users never pay for a job that didn't deliver.","demo")
def gx(h): return h/24*100
cl=[(9.0,9.8),(13.0,13.55),(14.9,15.7)]
on=''.join(f"<div class='on' style='left:{gx(a):.2f}%;width:{gx(b-a):.2f}%;--d:{k}'></div><div class='tail' style='left:{gx(b):.2f}%;width:{gx(.12):.2f}%'></div>" for k,(a,b) in enumerate(cl))
add("P3","Behind the scenes",pad("<div class='fill'>"+R(0,"<h1>We pay for GPU minutes<br><em>only when someone is waiting.</em></h1>")+R(1,"<div class='strip'><div class='row'><span class='lab'>Owned GPU</span><div class='track'><div class='own'></div></div></div><div class='row'><span class='lab hl'>NeuroLens</span><div class='track'>"+on+"</div></div><div class='axis'><span></span><div><span>00:00</span><span>06:00</span><span>12:00</span><span>18:00</span><span>24:00</span></div></div></div>")+"<div class='cols c3' style='gap:70px;margin-top:70px'>"+R(2,"<h2 style='font-size:44px'>Check first</h2><p style='font-size:28px'>Length, size and credit are verified before any GPU wakes.</p>")+R(3,"<h2 style='font-size:44px'>Scale to zero</h2><p style='font-size:28px'>Off after 7 idle minutes: about $5 a month when idle.</p>")+R(4,"<h2 style='font-size:44px'>Refund by default</h2><p style='font-size:28px'>Credit is charged or refunded exactly once. A failed job costs nothing.</p>")+"</div></div>"),
"Briefly, here's what happens behind the scenes. Analysing a video takes minutes, longer than a web request can wait, so the website never waits for the GPU. Your browser uploads straight to S3, a queue holds the notice, and a Lambda function checks the video before handing it to a SageMaker endpoint. On this illustrative day, an owned GPU, the grey bar, is paid for around the clock. Ours, the orange blocks, starts only when a video is waiting and stops after seven idle minutes, so the service costs about five dollars a month when idle. Credit is reserved, then charged or refunded exactly once, so a failed job costs nothing. The full architecture is in our report. I'll pass to my colleague, who'll explain the business behind it.")
# ---------------- P4 ----------------
add("P4","Business model",pad("<div class='fill'>"+R(0,"<h1>Pay as you go. <em>Credit, not cards.</em></h1>")+"<div class='cols c2' style='margin-top:100px'>"+R(1,"<p class='mono hl'>Who and how much</p><h2 style='margin-top:14px'>$1.00 per started 30 s</h2><p>For independent creators and small agencies picking one of many AI-made cuts. New users get $10 of credit: ten videos.</p>")+R(2,"<p class='mono hl'>Why prepaid credit</p><h2 style='margin-top:14px'>Card fees are fixed</h2><p>About 2.9% + $0.30 a charge: a third of a $1.00 sale, but only 4% of a $20 pack.</p>")+"</div>"+R(3,"<p class='sub' style='margin-top:90px;font-size:34px'>Course version: an administrator grants credit; Stripe Checkout belongs to the <span class='hl'>production design</span>. TRIBE v2 is CC BY-NC, so a paid launch needs a licence.</p>")+"</div>"),
"Thanks. Let's talk about the business model. NeuroLens is for independent creators and small agencies who make many AI-generated versions of an advert and need to pick one before paying for media. It's pay as you go: users buy prepaid credit and pay one dollar for each started thirty seconds of video. New users get ten dollars of invite credit, enough for ten thirty-second videos. Why credit rather than charging a card per video? A card fee of about 2.9 percent plus thirty cents would take about a third of a one-dollar charge, but only about four percent of a twenty-dollar pack. In our proof of concept an administrator grants the credit; card payment belongs to the production design. One caveat: TRIBE v2 is released for non-commercial use, so a paid launch would need a licence from Meta, or a different model.")
add("P4","Unit economics",pad("<div style='display:grid;grid-template-columns:760px 1fr;gap:100px;flex:1;align-items:center'><div>"+R(0,"<h1>What one analysis <em>costs us.</em></h1>")+R(1,"<div class='big' style='font-size:150px;margin-top:70px'>$0.14–0.23</div><p class='sub' style='font-size:36px;margin-top:16px'>our AWS cost per 30 s video, against a $1.00 price</p>")+"</div>"+R(2,"<div class='cost'><div class='r'><span>GPU: 393 s at $1.22/h (L4)</span><span>$0.13</span></div><div class='r'><span>Start-up + idle, per session</span><span>7.5 + 7 min</span></div><div class='r'><span>Cost, 3 videos per session</span><span>$0.23</span></div><div class='r'><span>Cost, 30 videos per session</span><span>$0.14</span></div><div class='r'><span>Fixed cost per month</span><span>$5</span></div><div class='r t'><span>Gross margin</span><span>77 – 86%</span></div></div><p class='src'>Report, Table 6. Cost per video = GPU time × hourly price + (start-up + idle) × hourly price ÷ videos per session. Spot GPUs (≈ $0.10 a video) were not available.</p>")+"</div>"),
"What does one analysis cost us? Each video needs about 393 seconds of GPU time on the L4 we were given, at one dollar twenty-two an hour. That is about thirteen cents. On top of that, each session pays for one GPU start-up plus seven idle minutes, shared across its videos. If a team sends three videos in a session, we pay twenty-three cents a video. If it sends thirty, fourteen cents. Fixed costs are about five dollars a month. Against a one-dollar price, that is a gross margin of seventy-seven to eighty-six percent. We hoped for ten cents on Spot GPUs, but Spot capacity was not available.","data")
add("P4","SaaS vs on-premise",pad(R(0,"<h1>Cloud is cheaper below about <em>670 videos a month.</em></h1>")+"<div class='chart reveal fill' data-viz='break' style='--d:1'><div class='vz'></div></div>"),
"Why cloud rather than buying a GPU? We priced a machine with one RTX 4090 at five thousand dollars, with a three-year life. The model peaked at 19.9 gigabytes, so a 24 gigabyte card is enough. With idle power, that is about one hundred fifty-nine dollars a month, flat, and almost nothing per video, about a third of a cent of electricity. AWS has no hardware cost and about five dollars a month fixed, but twenty-three cents a video. The lines cross at 670 videos a month. Below that, renting by the hour is cheaper, and ad testing comes in bursts: thirty versions one week, none the next month. Above it, owning wins: at a thousand videos a month over three years, that is about fifty-eight hundred dollars owned against eighty-five hundred on AWS. The price of renting is the wait, which my colleague will show.","data")
add("P4","Limits",pad("<div class='fill'>"+R(0,"<h1>What it claims, <em>and what it doesn't.</em></h1>")+"<div class='cols c2' style='margin-top:90px;gap:100px'>"+R(1,"<p class='mono hl' style='margin-bottom:18px'>What it is</p><ul class='list'><li>An average viewer's predicted brain response</li><li>One scale for every video</li><li>A virtual pre-test for editing</li></ul>")+R(2,"<p class='mono' style='margin-bottom:18px'>What it isn't</p><ul class='list no'><li>A prediction of views or sales</li><li>Instant: the first video after a quiet spell waits ≈ 6 min</li><li>Cleared for commercial use (CC BY-NC)</li><li>Statistically proven (small test)</li></ul>")+"</div></div>"),
"We also want to be clear about what we don't claim. NeuroLens predicts an average viewer's brain response, second by second, on one scale for every video. It does not predict views or sales, and we did not test that. It isn't instant: after a quiet spell, the first video waits about six minutes for a GPU to start. It isn't cleared for commercial use, because the model licence is non-commercial. And our usability study was a small formative test, enough to find the main problems but not enough for statistical claims. Now I'll pass to my colleague, who'll show you how we tested it.")
# ---------------- P5 ----------------
add("P5","Latency",pad(R(0,"<h1>Where does the time <em>go in one job?</em></h1>")+R(1,"<p class='sub' style='margin-top:14px;font-size:32px'>Same code, same 30 s clip, on our own RTX 4090 and on AWS.</p>")+"<div class='chart reveal fill' data-viz='latency' style='--d:2'><div class='vz'></div><div class='lg'></div></div>"+R(3,"<p class='mono'>GPU time per video-second: 3.71 s (RTX 4090) vs 10.7 s (A10G) · first video after 7+ min idle: 779 s on AWS · same output on both</p>")),
"Thanks. We evaluated the system in three ways. First, speed. We ran the same code and the same thirty-second clip on a machine we own and on AWS. The GPUs differ, so GPU work is compared per second of video: 3.7 seconds on the RTX 4090, against 10.7 on an A10G on AWS. End to end, a warm thirty-second clip takes 128 seconds on our machine and 415 on AWS. Upload takes about three seconds, the wait before the GPU starts about nineteen, and the rest is the model. After a long idle, the first video takes about 779 seconds, because a GPU has to start. Both setups give the same result: across all eleven series, with and without sound, no value differs by more than 0.005.","data")
add("P5","Scaling",pad(R(0,"<h1>Five people <em>at once.</em></h1>")+R(1,"<p class='sub' style='margin-top:14px;font-size:32px'>Five uploads at once. The endpoint is set to one GPU, so jobs run one after another.</p>")+"<div class='chart reveal fill' data-viz='scaling' style='--d:2'><div class='vz'></div><div class='lg'><span><i style='background:#FF8A2A'></i>AWS, finish time of each job</span><span><i style='background:#8B929C'></i>On-premise, last job</span></div></div>"+R(3,"<p class='mono'>Seconds until each job finishes. No job was dropped. In 77 of 80 automated refund tests a failed job was refunded exactly once.</p>")),
"Second, load. What happens when several people upload at once? We sent five uploads at the same time. Both setups run one job at a time, so the jobs queue. On AWS they finished about 323 seconds apart, the last after 2,145 seconds. On our machine the last finished after 575. Nothing was dropped. For failures, in 77 of our 80 automated refund tests a failed or killed job was refunded exactly once; the other three stopped on a GPU-library error during setup. We did not kill a job on the live deployment. More simultaneous users would need a GPU quota increase.","data")
add("P5","Usability",pad("<div style='display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:100px;flex:1;align-items:center' data-viz='sus'><div>"+R(0,"<h1>Can people <em>use it?</em></h1>")+R(1,"<p class='sub'>Five student creators. One task: upload an ad, find one peak and one drop-off.</p>")+"</div>"+R(2,"<div class='stat'><span class='sc'>[TBD]</span><small> / 100</small></div><p class='mono' style='margin-top:10px'>SUS score · 68 is average · 80 is an A</p><div class='dots'></div><p class='mono'>completed unaided, of 5 · median time on task [TBD]</p>")+"</div>"),
"Third, usability. We ran a study with five participants from our target group, student creators. Each one signed in, uploaded a video, watched it process, and then used the timeline to find one moment where the response peaks and one where it drops. We measured task success and time on task, and gave them the System Usability Scale questionnaire. The average product scores sixty-eight, and eighty is the threshold for an A grade. Our average score was [TBD]. [TBD] of five completed the task without help, in a median of [TBD] seconds. One participant said: [TBD quote]. The biggest issue they raised was [TBD], and we've recorded it as future work.","data")
add("P5","Conclusion",pad("<div class='fill'>"+R(0,"<h1>A $10,000 brain study,<br><em>in 7 minutes for $1.</em></h1>")+R(1,"<p class='sub'>Live today: sign-in, upload, prediction, 3D brain, AI report, comparison, refunds.</p>")+"<div class='flow reveal' style='grid-template-columns:repeat(3,1fr);margin-top:110px;--d:2'><div class='n'><i>Next</i><b>Licence</b><span>TRIBE v2 is CC BY-NC: a paid launch needs a licence.</span></div><div class='n'><i>Next</i><b>Scale</b><span>A GPU pool that grows with the queue: cheaper above about 1,000 videos a month.</span></div><div class='n'><i>Next</i><b>Polish</b><span>Fix what our usability study flagged.</span></div></div></div>"),
"To wrap up. NeuroLens predicts a viewer's brain response to a video and returns it in about seven minutes for about one dollar, where an fMRI study costs ten thousand dollars or more and takes weeks. Below 670 videos a month, renting a GPU costs less than owning one. The trade-off is the wait: after a quiet spell, the first video waits about six minutes for a GPU. The model predicts an average viewer, not views or sales. Looking ahead: a commercial licence; a production design that scales with the queue, which we estimate pays off above about a thousand videos a month but have not built; and fixing what our usability study found. Thank you for listening. We'd be happy to take your questions.")
add("P5","Q&amp;A",pad("<div class='fill'>"+R(0,"<h1>Thank you.<br><em>Questions?</em></h1>")+R(1,"<p class='hl2' style='font-size:60px;margin-top:70px;font-weight:500'>df6girqyykyfa.cloudfront.net</p>")+R(2,"<p class='mono' style='margin-top:18px'>Code · github.com/kelvinlowhm86/neurolens</p>")+"</div>"),
"Open for questions. Keep answers ready on: why this model and its licence, what the prediction does and does not mean, the cloud design and the cold-start wait, why SageMaker rather than Spot or our own GPU pool (backup slide), how refunds work, and what the experiments showed.","title")
add("ALL","Backup",pad("<div class='fill'>"+R(0,"<h1>Recorded demo</h1>")+R(1,"<p class='sub'>[Embed the recorded demo of slides 8–11 here]</p>")+"</div>"),
"Press B. Say: 'The live site is having trouble, so here is the recording from this morning.' Then continue with the same talking points as the live demo.","demo")
add("ALL","Backup: production design",pad("<div class='fill'>"+R(0,"<h1>Beyond the proof of concept: <em>what we learned.</em></h1>")+"<div class='cols c3' style='gap:70px;margin-top:80px'>"+R(1,"<h2 style='font-size:44px'>Spot did not deliver</h2><p style='font-size:28px'>All 26 Spot launches failed for lack of capacity. Spot averaged only 1–9% below on-demand.</p>")+R(2,"<h2 style='font-size:44px'>A smaller GPU fits</h2><p style='font-size:28px'>The model peaked at 19.9 GB, so a 24 GB L4 or A10G is enough.</p>")+R(3,"<h2 style='font-size:44px'>The GPU sets the speed</h2><p style='font-size:28px'>119 s clip: 491 s on an L40S, 1,540 s on an L4.</p>")+"</div>"+R(4,"<p class='sub' style='margin-top:80px;font-size:34px'>Production design (EC2 GPU pool behind a load balancer): <span class='hl'>$0.10 – $0.20</span> a video, about $70 a month more fixed cost. Cheaper than SageMaker on the same L40S above about 480–1,440 videos a month. Not built: estimates.</p>")+"</div>"),
"Backup for questions about scaling. The preliminary report described a larger design: stateless Flask containers on Fargate behind a load balancer, and a pool of EC2 GPU workers that scales with the queue backlog. We built the Auto Scaling group with CPU instances, then moved the GPU to SageMaker after three findings. Spot did not deliver: all 26 launches failed for lack of capacity, and Spot was only one to nine percent cheaper. A smaller GPU is enough, since the model peaked at about twenty gigabytes. And the GPU sets the speed: an L40S finished a 119 second clip in 491 seconds against 1,540 on an L4. SageMaker charges about forty percent more per GPU-hour than EC2 for the same L40S, but the production design adds about seventy dollars a month of fixed cost. It only becomes cheaper above roughly a thousand videos a month, and we have not measured its start-up time. At today's volume, SageMaker is the better choice.","demo")
# ---------------- build ----------------
esc=lambda x: html.escape(x,quote=True)
words=lambda s: len(re.findall(r"\S+",s))
N=len(S); out=[]; per={}
for i,(p,l,b,sc,k) in enumerate(S,1):
    w=words(sc); sec=round(w/WPM*60); per.setdefault(p,[0,0]); per[p][0]+=w; per[p][1]+=sec
    kick=f"<div class='kicker'>{i:02d} · {l}</div>" if l else ""
    out.append(f"<section class='slide {k}'>{kick}<div class='who'>{p}</div>{b}<div class='pgn'>{i}/{N}</div><template><span class='m'>{p} · {w} words · ~{sec}s</span> {esc(sc)}</template></section>")
open('Slides.html','w').write(open('template.html').read().replace('%%SLIDES%%','\n'.join(out)).replace('%%DATA%%',json.dumps(DATA)))
md=["# NeuroLens: presentation script\n",f"Target: 450-500 words per presenter (~{WPM} words/min). Slides 1-20 are the talk, 21 is Q&A, 22-23 are backups (recorded demo, production design). `[TBD]` = fill from the usability study (edit DATA in gen.py for charts). Numbers come from the final report.\n"]
cur=None
for i,(p,l,b,sc,k) in enumerate(S,1):
    if p!=cur: md.append(f"\n## {p}: {per[p][0]} words, ~{per[p][1]//60}:{per[p][1]%60:02d}\n"); cur=p
    md.append(f"**Slide {i}: {l or 'Title'}** ({words(sc)} words)\n\n> {sc}\n")
tw=sum(v[0] for k,v in per.items() if k!='ALL'); ts=sum(v[1] for k,v in per.items() if k!='ALL')
md.append(f"\n**Total: {tw} words, ~{ts//60}:{ts%60:02d}**\n"); open('Script.md','w').write('\n'.join(md))
for p,v in per.items(): print(p,v[0],'words',v[1],'s')
print('total',tw,ts//60,ts%60)
